111:**D152 — DEVDESK-470: the Diagnostics form gets a Trace Path sub-level that reuses the Path tab's engine. Decided (user, 2026-11-15): (1) The sub-level of the Diagnostics form runs on the single `m.pathModel` instance, not a second one. A second instance would accept `pathResolvedMsg`, `pathHopMsg` and `pathDoneMsg` (which carry only the generation, with counters at 0) in both places. Cost, accepted: the target field of the Path tab receives the form's target, as `H` does, and any text being typed there is overwritten, until P2-02 (removal of the Path tab). *Ended by D153 point 4.* (2) No `ctrl+o` and no relaunch inside the sub-level: to relaunch, `esc` then activate Trace Path again. *Superseded by D153 point 3: `ctrl+o` and `enter` (relaunch) work inside the trace level.* (3) `enter` on a text field always runs Run Diagnostics; a trace starts only from its button. (4) When Port or DNS Server is filled in, the trace runs on the target alone, with an Info notice in the footer. (5) The refusal of Run Diagnostics is unchanged (a timed error, no underline). Only Trace Path applies Rule 140 (invalid field underlined, error also in the footer). (6) The Trace Path button is greyed, with its reason in the footer permanently, when the platform does not support the trace (DEVDESK-334). Reference: D150 point 5 (the net view redesign, option P2, comes after DEVDESK-335). Why: one engine, one generation counter, so the two views cannot answer for each other. No ADR: local and reversible. **Plane:** DEVDESK-470, decided by the maintainer on 2026-11-15. Shipped with DEVDESK-470. Sources: `internal/ui/netdiag/trace_form.go`, `trace_level.go`, `.claude/plans/net-view-ux-audit.md`, D150, D151.**
113:**D151 — DEVDESK-474: a proven absence of route is Fail; it refines D20 and does not contradict it. Decided (user, 2026-11-15): (1) The `route` stage of Diagnostics (`internal/netcheck/stage_route.go`) classifies each lookup error with `nettrace.IsNoRoute`. If every looked-up address is proven without a route, the verdict is Fail; the results header and the MCP verdict go from UNKNOWN to FAIL in that case. (2) D20 stands for everything else: a lookup that could not answer is Unknown when no address is routed, and when some are routed it is OK with reason `route-undetermined`. Warn `partial-route` counts only the proven addresses. (3) Only the first 8 addresses (`maxRoutedAddresses`) are looked up. (4) The system error text goes in the facts `No route` and `Route undetermined`, never in the summary (Rule 129). (5) Known limits: the proof exists on Linux and WSL2 only; on Windows (`windows.Errno` not recognized) and macOS (error without errno) the result stays `undetermined`; if `go-netroute` stopped wrapping the netlink errno with `%w`, Linux would fall back to `undetermined` silently; `prohibit` routes (`EACCES`) stay `undetermined`. (6) Follow-up: a story to create for Windows and macOS. Documented in `docs/architecture/network.md`, Diagnostics tab.**
115:**D150 — DEVDESK-335 (S-03c): the Path tab refuses a port in the target field; it does not accept and ignore it. Decided: (1) The target takes a host or an IP address only. `host:port` is refused before any probe: the footer shows `Path ignores ports: enter a host or IP address only` at level Error, and no trace starts. Nothing is sent. (2) The `pathTarget.Port` field is removed, with its display in the header. The probes stay UDP to port 33435, fixed (D143). (3) IPv6 stays refused, unchanged. (4) Supersedes in part D144 point (3), which validated and showed the port (option A). Points (1), (2), (4) and (5) of D144 stand. (5) The redesign of the net view (Path under Diagnostics, one input, audit `.claude/plans/net-view-ux-audit.md`, option P2) comes after DEVDESK-335, in a separate story not yet filed in Plane. Why: the story requires showing only what is observed; accepting a value the engine ignores misleads the user, and the port looked measured when it was not. Hypothesis (prudent default, not validated): a trailing `:` with an empty port is refused too. No ADR: local and reversible. **Plane:** DEVDESK-335, decided by the maintainer on 2026-11-15 during DEVDESK-335. Sources: that decision (conversation, not a PR), D143, D144, worktree `.worktrees/feat/devdesk-335-path-failure` (uncommitted when this entry was written).**
125:**D145 — DEVDESK-329 (S-04; superseded in part by D155: the rejected alternative of point 1, reusing the Diagnostics result, is adopted): the Path column starts with this machine, its interface and its gateway. Decided: (1) The route (interface, source address, gateway) is read by `netcheck.Env.Route` in the trace producer, for the traced address, and carried by `pathResolvedMsg`. The `route` stage is not re-run and its facts (display strings) are not parsed. Rejected: re-running the stage and parsing its facts (parses display text); the local address of the trace socket, `getsockname` in `nettrace` (no interface name, no gateway, and it widens `nettrace` for a UI concern); reusing the Diagnostics result on the `H` path (stale, exists only on that path, and Diagnostics may route several addresses where the trace uses the first). No new message: the result rides on `pathResolvedMsg`, so the channel capacity is unchanged. go-netroute asks the kernel the question `ip route get` asks (policy rules applied), and the trace socket is unmarked and unbound, so the interface row and the probes agree; under a full-tunnel VPN the row names the tunnel. (2) One row type, `pathStep`, four kinds (source, interface, gateway, hop); `pathSteps` is the only place that orders the column. (3) The source row is always `this machine`, never `os.Hostname()`: under WSL2 the distro host name defaults to the Windows computer name and would label the source as another machine. Its address is on the interface row. (4) Column 2 is renamed `Answer` → `Step`: `source`, `interface` and `gateway` are not answers. (5) A route lookup error is not fatal: the rows read `undetermined`, the trace still runs, and the raw error goes to the log only (`ERROR [netdiag/path] route …`). (6) Nothing is shown before the route is known (empty table, `Resolving …`); the source row appears with the first row. (7) The footer counter counts hop rows only. (8) A trace error clears every row, as DEVDESK-328 did; reading failures is S-09. Hypotheses (prudent defaults, not validated): local rows have an empty Hop and RTT, not `-` (`-` means no answer); the gateway row stays when it repeats hop 1; no gateway reads `directly connected`; no interface name reads `unnamed (address)`. Why: the plan `.claude/plans/devdesk-329-path-column-plan.md` (worktree `.worktrees/feat/devdesk-329-path-column`, uncommitted when this entry was written); acceptance criteria 1 to 5 of Plane DEVDESK-329. No ADR: local, reversible, one view, no public API. **Plane:** DEVDESK-329 (S-04), dated 2026-11-14. Sources: the plan above, D144, D-a to D-d of the plan.**
127:**D144 — DEVDESK-328 (S-03b; point 3 superseded in part by D150): the path trace has a fifth tab, Path, in the network view. Decided: (1) Path is the fifth tab, last. *Superseded by D153 point 1: the Path tab is removed.* (2) Streaming is a callback of the engine, `nettrace.TraceEach(ctx, target, opts, onHop)`, not a field of `Options`, which stays comparable (`Trace` calls it with nil). The engine may report a hop above the terminal one, so the UI reconciles with `Result.Hops` at done. (3) The target field takes `host` or `host:port`: the port is validated and shown in the header, but has no effect on the probes, which stay UDP to port 33435 (option A). *Superseded in part by D150: a port is now refused, not shown.* (4) `H` (`keymap.Diagnose`) on the Local route row of Diagnostics opens Path prefilled with the host only, and never launches; `enter` launches, `esc` cancels. *Its Path tab is replaced by the trace level (D153 point 2); the rest stands.* (5) Error labels are fixed texts; the system error text goes to the log only. The body is always the hop table; the target sits in the bar slot. Hypotheses (prudent defaults, not validated): 30 hops, 3 s per probe and 30 s overall (engine defaults); the first IPv4 address when a name resolves to several; `enter` greyed outside supported Linux and refused with a Warn. Why: the plan `.claude/plans/devdesk-328-path-tab-plan.md` (main checkout) keeps `Options` comparable and gives the view a single streamed source; `H` prefills without sending, since nothing leaves before an explicit `enter`. No ADR: local and reversible. **Plane:** DEVDESK-328 (S-03b), dated 2026-11-14. Sources: the plan above, D143, worktree `.worktrees/feat/devdesk-328-path-tab` (uncommitted when this entry was written).**

## Brouillon de décision pour `docs/backlog.md` (à archiver par l'archivist, numéro suivant : D157)

> **D157 — Banc Nexus (NX-01) : outil Go dans `internal/nexusbench`, descripteur versionné, secrets
> hors du dépôt.** Contexte : NX-01 demande un Nexus Community jetable sur le k0s de `datomir`,
> déployé, provisionné et détruit en une commande, sans toucher la suite de tests ni la CI.
> Décision : un `package main` Go (`internal/nexusbench/cmd/nexus-bench`) pilote `kubectl` (pas de
> `client-go`) et l'API REST de Nexus, appelé par des tâches `mise run nexus-bench:*` hors `ci` ;
> manifestes `text/template` embarqués, valeurs validées ; namespace `nexus-bench`, NodePorts
> 30481/30482, bloc 30483-30489 réservé ; descripteur JSON déterministe versionné
> (`internal/nexusbench/descriptor.json`), secrets sous `$XDG_STATE_HOME/devdesk/nexus-bench/`,
> partagés par les worktrees ; destruction par suppression du seul namespace étiqueté ;
> acceptation de l'EULA Community seulement sur demande explicite (`NEXUS_BENCH_ACCEPT_EULA=yes`) ;
> tests d'intégration gardés par `DEVDESK_NEXUS_BENCH=1`. Rejeté : scripts bash (non testables en
> unitaire dans ce dépôt), `client-go` (poids dans le `go.mod` du binaire), descripteur ignoré par
> git (NX-08 ne pourrait plus détecter une version changée sans le banc). Conséquences : NX-02 ajoute
> l'amont (`bench-upstream`, port 5000) et un NodePort du bloc réservé ; NX-03 à NX-08 lisent le
> descripteur par `nexusbench.LoadDescriptor`.
